Program RepeatBugCheck;
// Bug 2.23: a program/procedure/variable name starting with "repeat"
// (case-insensitive) can falsely trigger the inline-assembler `repeat N`/
// `repend` unroll parser. Orgasm::ProcessUnrolling scans the generated
// assembly text line by line and treats ANY line whose simplified,
// lowercased text starts with "repeat" as the opening of an unroll block
// (a bare startsWith("repeat") check, no word-boundary/shape check).
// Asm6502::Program emits the program's own name as a bare assembly label
// at the very top of the generated code; since this program is named
// "RepeatBugCheck", that label line itself gets misidentified as an
// unroll directive, fails to parse a valid repeat count from it, and
// throws - even though this program never uses `repeat N`/`repend`
// unrolling at all. The ordinary Pascal `repeat...until` loop below is
// completely unrelated to this feature and compiles/runs fine on its own
// merits; it's only the PROGRAM NAME that trips the bug.
//
// Method B (compile-time), no VICE step. Confirmed live: fails during
// assembly (not parsing) with "Repeat count must be either 1 or
// 2-dimensional." - the exact documented message. Confirmed control:
// renaming only the program to anything not starting with "repeat"
// (tested: "NotTheBug") makes this identical source compile clean, so
// the fix direction (require the line to actually match
// `repeat\s+\d+(\s+\d+)?$` rather than a bare prefix check) should make
// this program-name spelling compile clean too, still producing a
// correct repeat...until loop.

var
	i : byte;

begin
	i := 0;
	repeat
		i := i + 1;
	until i = 3;
	screen_bg_col := i;
	loop();
end.
